WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.920
Tooling helps find bounds defects earlier, but it does not replace clear contracts.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:13.300
Compiler warnings, static analysis, sanitizers, assertions, and boundary-focused tests all see different parts of the problem.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:21.120
The strongest practice is to combine them with APIs that carry size information and code review that traces how that size is used.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:31.420
Compiler warnings can catch suspicious patterns such as signed and unsigned comparisons, array bounds concerns, truncation, ignored return values, and questionable conversions.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:39.000
Warnings should be treated as review items, especially when a warning appears near indexing, copying, formatting, or parser advancement.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.860
Static analysis can identify risky flows and suspicious indexes across functions.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:52.080
It can follow input-derived lengths, offsets, pointer parameters, and capacity values through code paths that are tedious to inspect manually.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:58.220
Static analysis is most useful when the codebase has consistent naming and helper patterns that make intent visible.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.000
Runtime sanitizers can expose out-of-bounds reads and writes during testing.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:15.060
They are especially valuable when tests cover empty buffers, single-element buffers, maximum accepted sizes, one-past-boundary values, malformed records, missing terminators, and resize failures.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.600
A sanitizer finding should become both a fix and a regression test.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.540
Assertions can document invariants during development.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:31.800
They can express assumptions such as index less than count, count no greater than capacity, terminator space available, or offset plus length within range.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:39.220
Assertions are not a substitute for runtime validation of external input, but they make internal assumptions visible and easier to test.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.440
Fuzzing and boundary-focused tests can exercise unusual sizes and inputs at a safe defensive level.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.580
The goal is to find crashes, unexpected behavior, and missing rejection paths before release.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:58.040
Code review should then connect those findings back to API contracts, helper functions, and documented bounds rules.
